    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A portable solar panel only has to do two things: make as much power as its size allows, and deliver it in a form your power station will accept. Most buying mistakes are about the second part. Match the connector and stay under the voltage limit, and almost any good monocrystalline panel will serve you well.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Matters in a Portable Solar Panel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the wattage on the label and then discount it. A panel’s rating is measured under standard test conditions: bright light, a cool cell temperature and a perfect angle. Outside, panels run hot, the sun moves and the air is never perfectly clear, so a portable panel commonly delivers somewhere around 60-80% of its rating at midday in good conditions, and less at other times. That is normal, not a defect, and every brand is subject to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Almost every good portable solar panel now uses monocrystalline cells, with manufacturers commonly claiming efficiencies in the low-to-mid 20s percent. Efficiency does not change how many watts a 100W panel makes; it changes how big the panel has to be to make them. Higher efficiency means a smaller, lighter panel for the same rating, which is what you want in something you carry. Treat claims above about 25% for consumer folding panels with skepticism.
          </p>

          <p>
            Form factor is the next real decision. Fabric folding panels with ETFE or PET surfaces are the lightest per watt and fold flat, but they are more vulnerable to sharp objects and abrasion. Rigid suitcase and briefcase panels behind glass are heavier and bulkier but far tougher. Most power station owners are best served by a fabric folder; people charging 12V batteries in an RV or using panels on job sites often prefer a rigid suitcase.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is the part that decides whether the panel works at all: the connector and the voltage. A panel that will not physically plug into your station, or that produces more voltage than the station accepts, is useless until you buy an adapter or a different panel. That is the subject of the next section, and it is worth reading before you look at the picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have not picked a power station yet, it is worth doing that first, because the station sets the limits the panel has to fit. Our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> covers capacity and inverter sizing, and our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">solar generator guide</a> pairs specific stations with the panel wattage that refills them. This page focuses on the panel side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="connectors-voltage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Connectors, Voc and Input Limits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Portable panels use a handful of connectors, and stations use a slightly different handful. <strong>MC4</strong> is the industry standard: a locking, weather-sealed pair of plugs used on nearly every rooftop and most folding panels. <strong>Anderson Powerpole</strong> connectors appear on some Goal Zero gear and older Jackery panels. <strong>DC8020</strong> is the input on Jackery’s newer Explorer stations, <strong>DC7909</strong> (an 8mm barrel) shows up on older Jackery and many other stations, and <strong>XT60</strong> is the solar input on EcoFlow’s River and Delta lines.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is simple: buy a panel with MC4 output, or one made by your station’s brand. MC4 adapters to every common station input are cheap and widely sold, and most stations ship with one in the box. A proprietary panel can be the easiest option within its own brand and the most awkward outside it, which matters if you might switch stations later. Our <a href="/ecoflow-vs-jackery/">EcoFlow vs Jackery comparison</a> goes into the ecosystem side of that choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Voltage is the limit that can cause damage. Every panel lists an open-circuit voltage, or Voc: the voltage it produces with nothing connected. Every station lists a solar input voltage range. The panel’s Voc must stay below the station’s maximum, with margin, or the station may refuse to charge or, in the worst case, be damaged. Small stations often accept a lower maximum voltage than larger ones, so a panel that is perfect for a 2kWh station can be wrong for a 300Wh one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold weather raises Voc. Solar cells produce higher voltage when they are cold, so a panel that sits comfortably under your limit on a summer afternoon can creep over it on a clear, freezing morning. A rule of thumb is to keep the combined Voc at least 10-20% below the station’s maximum input voltage, more if you charge in winter. The station’s input current limit matters too, but exceeding it with panel watts is usually handled by the station simply capping the input; exceeding voltage is not.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Panel Voc, times the number in series, must stay well under the station’s max input voltage.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Read the Voc on the panel’s spec label and the solar input range in your station’s manual before you buy. Leave 10-20% headroom for cold mornings. If in doubt, wire in parallel or ask the manufacturer.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

