        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the station’s voltage limit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every station has a maximum solar input voltage, and panel open-circuit voltage adds up in series. Exceeding it can stop charging or damage the input. Add up the Voc of panels in series, compare it with the manual, and leave 10-20% headroom for cold mornings when voltage climbs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a panel with the wrong connector</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A proprietary output that does not match your station means another adapter, or no charging at all. MC4 is the safest choice because adapters to every common station input are cheap and widely available. If you buy a brand-specific panel, confirm the exact cable included for your station model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying the panel flat on the ground</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A flat panel loses a large share of its output whenever the sun is not overhead, which is most of the day and all winter. Use the kickstand, angle it toward the sun, and re-aim every couple of hours. A bifacial panel lying flat on the ground gets no rear-side benefit at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pairing a big battery with one small panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single 100W panel will not refill a 2kWh station in a day, or even two. Size panels to your daily use: roughly 100W for small stations, 200-400W around 1kWh, and 400W or more for larger units. Otherwise the battery slowly drains through an outage while the panel works flat out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving folding panels out in the rain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                IP ratings describe resistance to rain and splashes, not permanent outdoor exposure. Junction boxes and connectors are common failure points, and fabric panels age faster in constant weather. Bring folding panels in when you are done for the day, fold them dry, and store them in their case away from sharp objects.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

