        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use any solar panel with my portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, as long as two things match: the connector and the voltage. The panel’s open-circuit voltage, multiplied by the number in series, must stay below the station’s maximum solar input voltage with some headroom. Panels with MC4 output work with most stations through an adapter cable, and most stations include one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a portable solar panel take to charge a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the battery capacity by the panel’s real output. A 200W panel delivering 60-80% of its rating over five peak sun hours puts back roughly 600-800Wh on a clear summer day, which is most of a 1kWh station. Winter, clouds and poor aiming can halve that or worse, so plan with margin.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are bifacial solar panels worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are worth it if you prop them up over light-colored ground, where the back side catches reflected light. Over snow, sand or a white tarp the gain can be significant; over grass or dirt it is modest. If you mostly lay panels flat on a roof or truck bed, a good single-sided panel delivers nearly the same.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I connect two solar panels in series or parallel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Series is usually more efficient and starts charging earlier, but voltages add, so check the combined Voc against your station’s maximum. Parallel keeps voltage the same and adds current, which tolerates partial shade better but can exceed a station’s amp limit. If your station has two solar inputs, running one panel per input avoids the decision.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does Voc mean on a solar panel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Voc is the open-circuit voltage: the voltage the panel produces in sunlight with nothing connected. It is the highest voltage the panel will normally present to your station, and it rises in cold weather. Compare it with the station’s maximum solar input voltage, and leave 10-20% headroom for cold, clear mornings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can portable solar panels get wet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most can handle a rain shower while deployed, and many carry IP65, IP67 or IP68 ratings. That does not make them suitable for permanent outdoor installation. Connectors and junction boxes are the weak points, and fabric surfaces age faster in constant weather. Dry them before folding and store them indoors between uses.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts of solar do I need for a portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
